irst rectangle (300 \u00d7 700) is 210,000.\n- The area of the second rectangle (30 \u00d7 700) is 21,000.\n- The area of the third rectangle (30 \u00d7 50) is 1,500.\n- The area of the fourth rectangle (5 \u00d7 700) is 3,500.\n- The area of the fifth rectangle (5 \u00d7 50) is 250.\n\nFinally, add all these areas together to get the total area, which is the product of 335 and 750:\n\n```\n210,000\n+ 21,000\n+ 1,500\n+ 3,500\n+  250\n------\n251,250\n```\n\nTherefore, the product of 335 and 750 is 251,250.\n\n</think>.\n<answer>. Using the area model, we can break down the multiplication of 335 and 750 into smaller parts by representing each number as a sum of its place values. Here's how the area model looks:\n\n```\n  300 | 30 |  5\n+-----+-----+-----\n 700 | 21000 | 1500\n+-----+-----+-----\n  50 | 1500 |  25\n+-----+-----+-----\n```\n\nNow, let's calculate the area of each smaller rectangle:\n\n- The area of the first rectangle (300 \u00d7 700) is 210,000.\n- The area of the second rectangle (30 \u00d7 700) is 21,000.\n- The area of the third rectangle (30 \u00d7 50) is 1,500.\n- The area of the fourth rectangle (5 \u00d7 700) is 3,500.\n- The area of the fifth rectangle (5 \u00d7 50) is 250.\n\nAdding all these areas together gives us the total area, which is the product of 335 and 750:\n\n```\n210,000\n+ 21,000\n+ 1,500\n+ 3,500\n+  250\n------\n251,250\n```\n\nTherefore, the product of 335 and 750 is 251,250.</answer> </User|><|User|>That's clear. Can you also show me how to solve this using the partial products method?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c><think>.\nCertainly! The partial products method involves breaking down each number into its place values and then multiplying each part separately before adding the results. Here's how we can solve 335 \u00d7 750 using the partial products method:\n\n1. Break down 335 into 300, 30, and 5.\n2. Break down 750 into 700 and 50.\n3. Multiply each part of 335 by each part of 750 and then add the results.\n\nLet's go through